# The project video, as a cue sheet.
#
# Every frame is the real games being played by the real autopilot, rendered
# through the real cell shader -- this is the plugin running, not a mock-up.
# Render with:
#
#   ./build/coinopgl --sequence /tmp/co-frames --script tools/video.cues \
#                    --size 1920x1080 --seconds 51 --fps 30
#
# Syntax: `T Name=V` sets at a time, `T1..T2 Name=V1..V2` ramps between two
# (eased, because a parameter that starts and stops abruptly reads as a cut).
#
# Values are the 0..1 the host sees, except Game, Palette, Fit and Aspect, which
# are indices -- the same split the plugin itself has, see Controls.h.
#
# ## v0.2.0: this cut is the eight games that were added
#
# The v0.1.0 cut ran through the original five. Re-running that would be a video
# of the part of the plugin that did not change, so this one shows the new ones
# and leaves the first five to the release before it. The order is by how quickly
# each reads on screen: the maze first because everyone knows what a maze is, the
# fighter last because it is the one that needs a second to parse.
#
# Changing `Game` restarts the simulation (Sim::SetGame), so every segment is a
# game from its first tick. That is why Speed is pushed up in the segments whose
# interest accumulates -- Stacker has to build a stack and Trails has to lay
# trail, and neither has forty seconds to do it in.
#
# The pixel-map ending stays, because it is still the thing the plugin is *for*.

# ------------------------------------------------------------ 0s: Chase, Amber
# A maze carved fresh at every reset, four pursuers, and the loops that make
# being chased survivable. Amber because the pellets have to read as the thing
# you want and the pursuers as the thing you do not.
0.0   Game=6
0.0   Palette=1
0.0   Speed=0.62
0.0   Skill=0.88
0.0   Difficulty=0.45
0.0   Grid=0.415
0.0   Seed=0.25
0.0   Round=0.25
0.0   Gap=0.18
0.0   Glow=0.45
0.0   React=0.35

# -------------------------------------------------------- 6.5s: Stacker, Candy
# The falling-block game, and the one whose differences are the point: ten mixed
# polyominoes, and a clear rule that takes any contiguous run rather than a full
# row -- which is also the only rule that works on a playfield thirty cells wide.
# Speed high so a stack actually accumulates inside six seconds.
6.5   Game=5
6.5   Palette=3
6.5   Grid=0.30
6.5   Speed=0.88
6.5   Skill=0.45
6.5   Difficulty=0.55
6.5   Round=0.30
6.5   Gap=0.16
6.5   Glow=0.50

# ----------------------------------------------------------- 13s: Trails, Ice
# Four riders that never stop and never reverse. Deaths are resolved
# simultaneously, which is why a head-on kills both and not just the second one
# the loop happened to reach.
13.0  Game=9
13.0  Palette=2
13.0  Grid=0.415
13.0  Speed=0.80
13.0  Skill=0.80
13.0  Difficulty=1.0
13.0  Round=0.22
13.0  Gap=0.14
13.0  Glow=0.55

# -------------------------------------------------------- 19.5s: Girders, Fire
# Flat floors, alternating roll direction, hazards that cascade back down them.
# The slope in the original is what made barrels roll; at this resolution a slope
# is a staircase, so the direction alternates instead.
19.5  Game=7
19.5  Palette=5
19.5  Grid=0.35
19.5  Speed=0.70
19.5  Skill=0.85
19.5  Difficulty=0.75
19.5  Round=0.25
19.5  Gap=0.18
19.5  Glow=0.50

# ------------------------------------------------------ 25s: Swarm, Phosphor
# The formation does not descend. Attackers peel out of it one at a time, fly a
# curved run at the ship, and come back round to their own slot.
25.0  Game=8
25.0  Palette=0
25.0  Grid=0.33
25.0  Speed=0.55
25.0  Skill=0.80
25.0  Difficulty=0.65
25.0  Round=0.28
25.0  Gap=0.16
25.0  Glow=0.50
25.0  React=0.45

# --------------------------------------------------------- 30.5s: Reflex, Ice
# A hazard closes, a prompt opens, and the bar under the arrow is the remaining
# distance drawn out rather than a second clock counting the same thing.
30.5  Game=10
30.5  Palette=2
30.5  Grid=0.33
30.5  Speed=0.55
30.5  Skill=0.90
30.5  Difficulty=0.35
30.5  Round=0.25
30.5  Gap=0.18
30.5  Glow=0.45

# ------------------------------------------------------- 35.5s: Rafters, Mono
# Hit the floor from underneath and whatever is standing on it goes over. Mono
# because the mechanic is a silhouette -- upright or on its back -- and no hue
# is doing any of that work.
35.5  Game=11
35.5  Palette=4
35.5  Grid=0.35
35.5  Speed=0.60
35.5  Skill=0.90
35.5  Difficulty=0.60
35.5  Round=0.25
35.5  Gap=0.16
35.5  Glow=0.42

# ---------------------------------------------------------- 40s: Duel, Fire
# Wind-up, active, recovery. Only the active window is drawn, because drawing
# the wind-up would tell the other fighter exactly when to block.
40.0  Game=12
40.0  Palette=5
40.0  Grid=0.28
40.0  Speed=0.55
40.0  Skill=0.75
40.0  Difficulty=0.70
40.0  Round=0.30
40.0  Gap=0.18
40.0  Glow=0.55
40.0  React=0.45

# ------------------------------------------------ 44.5s: the pixel-map ending
# Drop the grid to nineteen cells across and open the gaps. This is what the
# plugin looks like when the composition is a fixture layout and Resolume's
# pixel mapper is sampling it.
#
# On Chase, and that is not an aesthetic choice. Changing `Game` restarts the
# simulation, and the ending needs a full field of cells on its first frame --
# Trails was tried here and a five-second-old light-cycle board is nearly black,
# which is the one thing this shot cannot be. Chase is dense from tick one
# because the maze starts full of pellets.
44.5  Game=6
44.5  Palette=5
44.5  Grid=0.415
44.5  Speed=0.55
44.5  Skill=0.90
44.5  Difficulty=0.35
44.5  Round=0.20
44.5  Gap=0.14
44.5  Glow=0.55

46.0..50.5  Grid=0.415..0.25
46.0..50.5  Gap=0.14..0.42
46.0..50.5  Round=0.20..1.00
46.0..50.5  Glow=0.55..0.80
